ults/' + $('#other_games_select').val();})"]] " | " [:a {:href "/event_names/379"} "Event type"]] [:h1 {:class "event_title"} "Double Sculls, Men" [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "20 – 27 July 1980"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/577"} "Grebnoy kanal, Sportivny komplex Krylatskoye, Moskva"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/158793"} "19 from 9 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "Norway’s participation in the American-led boycott meant that whoever won the men’s double sculls event at the 1980 Summer Olympics would earn a qualified victory at best. This was due to the fact that brothers " [:a {:href "/athletes/39084"} "Frank"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/39083"} "Alf Hansen"] ", the defending Olympic champions and winners of three of the last four World Championships, were excluded from competing. This meant that the only World Champion at the Games was " [:a {:href "/athletes/37203"} "Chris Baillieu"] " of Great Britain, one half of the 1977 championship duo and runner-up at the 1976 Olympics. Baillieu was considered a slight favorite, however, even without his traditional partner " [:a {:href "/athletes/37325"} "Mike Hart"] ", because he had won three of the last four Double Challenge Sculls at the Henley Royal Regatta. In Moscow he was partnered with " [:a {:href "/athletes/37259"} "Jim Clark"] ", a 1976 silver medalist in the coxed eights."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The opening round, however, was dominated by East Germany, who finished nearly five seconds ahead of the other heat winner, Great Britain. The German pair consisted of " [:a {:href "/athletes/37591"} "Joachim Dreifke"] ", bronze medalist from the 1976 Olympic single sculls and half of the 1975 World runners-up, and " [:a {:href "/athletes/37628"} "Klaus Kröppelien"] ", a successful international rower in other disciplines. In the final, the East Germans claimed gold by a comfortable margin, leaving silver and bronze for Yugoslavia and Czechoslovakia respectively. The former’s crew consisted of " [:a {:href "/athletes/41018"} "Zoran Pančić"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/41037"} "Milorad Stanulov"] ", a bronze medalist in the single sculls at the 1978 World Championships, while the latter was represented by " [:a {:href "/athletes/40048"} "Zdeněk Pecka"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/40094"} "Václav Vochoska"] ", both 1976 Olympic bronze medalists in the quadruple sculls. Great Britain, meanwhile, faltered in the final and finished fourth."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Pair"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/37591"} "Joachim Dreifke"] " / " [:a {:href "/athletes/37628"} "Klaus Kröppelien"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GDR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GDR.png"}] "GDR"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/41018"} "Zoran Pančić"] " / " [:a {:href "/athletes/41037"} "Milorad Stanulov"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/YUG"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/YUG.png"}] "YUG"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "3"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/40048"} "Zdeněk Pecka"] " / " [:a {:href "/athletes/40094"} "Václav Vochoska"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/TCH"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/TCH.png"}] "TCH"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "Bronze"} "Bronze"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "4"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athlete